; Pi 3 storage composition over the shared filesystem and file facade.
; Reader requests use the native ranged SD interface. The file facade holds
; Pi3StorageWriteBlocks as its writer and arms it only during a file operation.
; That wrapper additionally fences each block request to its exact range and
; closes the SD window afterwards. Block zero is never writable here.
;
; Boot confirmation mounts partition 1 without scanning free space. A/B record
; I/O is shared by all Pi 3 compositions in Lib/boot_record.pbi: existing,
; contiguous, 512-byte control files are replaced without allocation changes,
; under one-sector fences. Their allocation can occupy a larger cluster.
; Shared ab_record owns record validation, state changes, CRC and readback.
;
; Include after SDHOST, filesystem, hwfile, ab_record and boot_record.

Global gPi3StorageUp.i          ; 1 once a filesystem is mounted for us
Global gPi3StorageWhy.i         ; the refusal code from the last attempt
Global gPi3StoragePart.i        ; the partition FsMount() took
Global gPi3StorageInitAttempted.i ; one lazy SDHOST bring-up attempt

; THE THREE VALUES Anvil/Storage/hwfile.pbi READS, and nothing else. It
; declares them; this file is what sets them. gHwFileWriter is HELD, not
; installed - the shared seam arms it for the length of one write and
; disarms it on every path out, so between writes the filesystem has no
; way to write at all. 0 means this medium is readable and not writable.
;
; gPi3FileWriter is this board's own name for the same pointer, used by
; the A/B record callbacks at the foot of this file, which arm and disarm
; it themselves for the one write that decides whether the board boots
; this image again.
Global gPi3FileWriter.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pi3StorageCanWrite() - whether the native medium can accept writes.
;
;  Called by StorageUp() before anything writes, including the A/B
;  confirm. It answers 1 or 0; a 0 leaves the medium readable and
;  HwFileWritable() then says so, which is the honest state rather than a
;  write that fails half way through a settings file.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Pi3StorageCanWrite()
  ProcedureReturn Bool(p3sd_ready <> 0 And p3sd_blocks > 1)
EndProcedure

; The file facade arms this callback for one operation. Each block request
; additionally opens only its own SD range and closes it on success or failure.
Procedure.i Pi3StorageWriteBlocks(lba.i, count.i, buffer.i)
  Protected ok.i
  If Pi3StorageCanWrite() = 0 Or lba < 1 Or count < 1 : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  If lba >= p3sd_blocks Or count > p3sd_blocks - lba : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  If Pi3SdSetWriteWindow(lba, count) = 0 : ProcedureReturn 0 : EndIf
  ok = Pi3SdWriteBlocks(lba, count, buffer)
  Pi3SdSetWriteWindow(0, 0)
  ProcedureReturn ok
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  pi3StorageMountAny() - mount partition 1, and if that fails, any other.
;
;  PARTITION 1 FIRST AND BY NAME, not "the first one that mounts". A path
;  with no N: prefix means partition 1, so a board that quietly mounted
;  partition 2 because partition 1 was damaged would open a DIFFERENT
;  file for the same name - including the settings file, which is how a
;  board would come up with no network credentials and no complaint.
;
;  The fallback still exists, and it prints. A card whose boot partition
;  will not mount is a card an operator needs to look at, and a monitor
;  that can still read the data partition is a monitor they can look at
;  it FROM.
;
;  This is RaspberryPi4/Board/storage.pi4's storage_MountAny() with the
;  medium name folded in, because there is only one medium here.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i pi3StorageMountAny()
  Define n.i
  If FsSelectPartition(1) <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  PrintN("!! partition 1 of the card could not be mounted, so a path with no N:")
  PrintN("   prefix will not open. The other partitions are tried next.")
  Print("   The filesystem said: ")
  UartWriteStr(FsErrorText())
  PrintNl()
  n = 2
  While n <= #FS_MAX_PARTITION
    If FsSelectPartition(n) <> 0
      ProcedureReturn 1
    EndIf
    n = n + 1
  Wend
  PrintN("!! no partition on the card could be mounted at all. Nothing that reads")
  PrintN("   or writes a file will work this boot. The card is the one the board")
  PrintN("   booted from, so the loader could read it - which points at the")
  PrintN("   partition table or at a filesystem this monitor does not recognise")
  PrintN("   rather than at the medium.")
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE CONFIRM PATH IS SIZED IN CARD READS, NOT IN LINES
; ======================================================================
;  MEASURED ON SILICON, 2026-09-18: a single-block read on this card
;  costs MORE THAN FOURTEEN MILLISECONDS, and the boot chain's own mount
;  spends 1,039 of them. The boot lane is chasing the per-read cost - a
;  status poll after every read is the lead - but until that lands, the
;  arithmetic is brutal and it decides the shape of this file: at 14 ms
;  a read, SEVENTY-FOUR READS IS A SECOND, and the loader's never-fed
;  watchdog gives this monitor FIFTEEN.
;
;  So the confirm does the LEAST CARD WORK THAT CAN POSSIBLY CONFIRM,
;  and everything else waits until the deadman is off:
;
;    IN THE WINDOW   mount partition 1; open the ONE control record the
;                    handoff page names; read it; rewrite it; read it
;                    back. Nothing else is opened, no directory is
;                    listed, the second partition is not touched and no
;                    report is printed.
;    AFTER IT        StorageUp() does the rest, lazily, the first time a
;                    command actually needs a file.
;
;  THE SECOND PARTITION IS THE ONE TO WATCH. Mounting exFAT means reading
;  its boot region and its allocation bitmap, and doing that inside the
;  window to be tidy would be how a board that works becomes a board that
;  resets every fifteen seconds with no explanation.
;
;  WHY NOT SIMPLY WIDEN THE WINDOW. It is the immutable loader's, it is
;  deliberately never fed, and it is the only thing that gets the board
;  back if this image is bad. A monitor that needed it widened would be
;  asking for the safety net to be raised because it was slow.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pi3StorageForConfirm() - the minimum: a reader, closed write window, and
;  partition 1.
;
;  IT PRINTS NOTHING ON THE WAY IN. Every line printed at 115200 is
;  another millisecond, and this runs against a clock.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Pi3StorageForConfirm()
  If gPi3StorageUp <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf
  If p3sd_ready = 0
    gPi3StorageWhy = -1
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  FsSetRangeReader(@Pi3SdReadBlocks)
  FsSetRangeWriter(0)
  Pi3SdSetWriteWindow(0, 0)
  If Pi3StorageCanWrite() <> 0
    gPi3FileWriter = @Pi3StorageWriteBlocks
    gHwFileWriter = @Pi3StorageWriteBlocks
    gHwFileWritable = 1
  Else
    gPi3FileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWritable = 0
  EndIf
  ; PARTITION 1 ONLY, and FsMount rather than the fallback walk: the
  ; control records are on the boot partition or they are nowhere, and
  ; trying three more partitions inside the window would cost more than
  ; it could ever find.
  If FsMount(1) = 0
    gHwFileMounted = 0
    gPi3FileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWritable = 0
    gPi3StorageWhy = FsLastError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  gPi3StoragePart = FsPartition()
  gPi3StorageUp = 1
  gHwFileMounted = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  StorageUp() - lazily bring up the medium and mount a filesystem.
;
;  The console is ready and the MMU's Device mappings are active before
;  this is called. The monitor remembered the clock properties before the
;  MMU; Pi3SdSetBootClock() supplies the current CORE rate so PIO init does
;  not need a firmware mailbox transaction. This is one attempt only.
;
;  After init, install this board's reader and held writer on the shared
;  layer, close the write window, and mount.
;
;  IT IS IDEMPOTENT. A command calls HwStorageUp() before it reads files
;  and has no idea whether another command did so first.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i StorageUp()
  If gPi3StorageUp <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 1
  EndIf

  ; Direct firmware boot does not touch the card before the console prompt.
  ; All firmware properties were collected before MmuUp(), so card init
  ; receives the remembered clock and performs only PIO register access.
  If p3sd_ready = 0 And gPi3StorageInitAttempted = 0
    gPi3StorageInitAttempted = 1
    If pi3_sd_clock_ceiling >= 1000000 And pi3_sd_core_actual >= 1000000
      If Pi3SdSetBootClock(pi3_sd_core_actual) <> 0
        If Pi3SdInit(pi3_sd_clock_ceiling, 0, pi3_ram_end) = 0
          gPi3StorageWhy = Pi3SdError()
        EndIf
      EndIf
    Else
      gPi3StorageWhy = -1
    EndIf
  EndIf

  If p3sd_ready = 0
    If gPi3StorageWhy = 0 : gPi3StorageWhy = Pi3SdError() : EndIf
    If gPi3StorageWhy = 0 : gPi3StorageWhy = -1 : EndIf
    PrintN("!! the SD host could not be initialized on demand, so no filesystem")
    PrintN("   is available this boot. The Anvil console remains usable.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; The reader is this board's. FsSetRange* installs into BOTH
  ; filesystems, so one call covers FAT32 and exFAT and they cannot end
  ; up with different block sources.
  FsSetRangeReader(@Pi3SdReadBlocks)

  ; The facade holds this bounded ranged writer; it is not armed here.
  ; Each physical request opens only its own nonzero block range and closes
  ; that range when it returns. Mount failures clear all writable state.
  FsSetRangeWriter(0)
  Pi3SdSetWriteWindow(0, 0)
  If Pi3StorageCanWrite() <> 0
    gPi3FileWriter = @Pi3StorageWriteBlocks
    gHwFileWriter = @Pi3StorageWriteBlocks
    gHwFileWritable = 1
  Else
    ; Readable, not writable. The pointer stays 0, HwFileWritable()
    ; answers 0, and a caller refuses loudly rather than finding out half
    ; way through a settings file.
    gPi3FileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWritable = 0
  EndIf

  If pi3StorageMountAny() = 0
    gHwFileMounted = 0
    gPi3FileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWriter = 0
    gHwFileWritable = 0
    gPi3StorageWhy = FsLastError()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  gPi3StoragePart = FsPartition()
  gPi3StorageUp = 1
  gHwFileMounted = 1
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwStorageUp()
  ProcedureReturn StorageUp()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwStorageReport() - the paragraph `help` and `info` print about where
;  files come from on this board.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwStorageReport()
  If gPi3StorageUp = 0
    PrintN("  Files come from the SD card the board booted from, through this")
    PrintN("  monitor's own host driver. Nothing is mounted yet - the first")
    PrintN("  command that needs a file will mount it and say so if it cannot.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  Print("  Files come from partition ")
  PrintDec(gPi3StoragePart)
  Print(" of the SD card the board booted from, ")
  If FsType() = 2
    Print("formatted exFAT")
  Else
    Print("formatted FAT32")
  EndIf
  PrintN(".")
  PrintN("  The card is read and written one block at a time by this monitor's own")
  PrintN("  host driver, and the filesystem code above it is the same code every")
  PrintN("  Anvil board uses - nothing about FAT32 or exFAT is written twice.")
  If gPi3FileWriter = 0
    PrintN("  It is MOUNTED READ-ONLY: the write window could not be opened, so")
    PrintN("  save, mkdir and rm will refuse rather than half-write a file.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("  Writes are allowed everywhere except the partition table in block 0,")
  PrintN("  which this monitor never has a reason to write and will not.")
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE A/B CONTROL RECORDS, REACHED BY NAME.
;
;  Anvil/Storage/ab_record.pbi holds the record's layout and its state
;  machine and knows nothing about a medium. These two procedures are the
;  medium half, and they are four filesystem calls each.
;
;  THE NAMES ARE ON THE BOOT PARTITION AND SAY SO. "1:" is the FAT32
;  partition the loader, the firmware and both slots live on; the data
;  partition is "2:" and has no business holding a boot-control record.
;  Writing the prefix rather than relying on the default means a monitor
;  that had to fall back to mounting partition 2 - because partition 1
;  would not mount - cannot then confirm a slot against a record that is
;  not there, or worse, one that is.
; ======================================================================







; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Pi3StorageConfirmSlot() - release the loader's deadman, or say why not.
;
;  THE MOST IMPORTANT FIFTEEN SECONDS THIS MONITOR HAS. The loader armed
;  a never-fed watchdog before it branched here and this is what turns it
;  off: confirm the running slot, then stop the timer. Miss it and the
;  board resets and falls back to the last confirmed image, which from
;  the other end of a serial cable looks exactly like this image having
;  crashed.
;
;  IT PRINTS ON EVERY FAILURE PATH AND IT PRINTS THE SENTENCE, because
;  the operator has a few seconds to read it before the reset, and the
;  difference between "the card is read-only" and "the handoff page is
;  not valid" is the difference between an afternoon and a minute.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i Pi3StorageConfirmSlot()
  If Pi3StorageForConfirm() = 0
    PrintN("!! the boot partition would not mount, so the running slot cannot be")
    PrintN("   confirmed and the loader's deadman is still armed. This board is")
    PrintN("   about to reset and fall back to its other slot.")
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  AbSetIo(@Pi3BootReadRecord, @Pi3BootWriteRecord)
  If AbConfirmRunning() = 0
    PrintN("!! the running slot could not be confirmed, so the loader's fifteen-")
    PrintN("   second deadman is still armed and this board is about to reset and")
    PrintN("   fall back to its other slot.")
    Print("   ")
    UartWriteStr(AbErrorText())
    PrintNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure
